# =============================================================================
#  Tessera's authorization, written down.
#
#  ⚠️ WHAT THIS FILE IS. It is the VOCABULARY — the floors, the permissions, and
#  what each role carries — and it is the SEED: at the first start `PolicySeedStep`
#  writes the roles, their bundles and the bootstrap assignment into `access_*`
#  rows, once, behind a per-step ledger. From then on THE ENGINE READS ROWS AND
#  NOTHING ELSE. `AccessStorageConfiguration` refuses the composition that would
#  otherwise union this document back over the tables, because a permission taken
#  off a role through the management screen must not go on being granted by the
#  file it was seeded from.
#
#  So: a line removed from this file is a row removed from the table, on the next
#  start whose checksum has moved. It is not a second opinion the engine consults.
#
#  ⚠️ WHAT IT REPLACED. Four tables — `permissions`, `project_roles`,
#  `project_role_permissions`, `project_permission_overrides` — and a resolver
#  that joined them by surrogate key. A permission is stored by NAME now, so the
#  first of those was a third copy of a list the code already states, and
#  `DeclaredPolicyValidator` checks this file against `Permissions` in both
#  directions: a name here no constant matches fails the boot, and so does a
#  constant no line here declares.
#
#  ⚠️ ONE SEMANTIC CHANGE, IN THE SAFE DIRECTION. Overrides used to be applied in
#  insertion order, so an ALLOW written after a DENY quietly won. The engine's rule
#  is DENY WINS, THE SUBTRACTION LAST, AT EVERY LEVEL — so the same two rows now
#  refuse. Any pair where that changes the answer was a pair nobody could have
#  predicted the answer to.
# =============================================================================

policy "tessera" {

    # ── The vocabulary ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    #
    # ⚠️ RENAMED FROM `BROWSE_PROJECT`, `CREATE_ISSUE` AND SO ON, and the grammar is
    # why rather than taste. A permission here must be `namespace:action` — the colon
    # is what the parser identifies the shape BY, since nothing else tells a
    # permission from one of the language's own keywords — so a single-word name
    # cannot be written down at all. `CursorMatcher` says outright that refusing MORE
    # than two segments would be "a language dictating a product's vocabulary"; the
    # floor of two is the same thing said quietly, and it is a library finding rather
    # than a Tessera preference. `V000013` maps the old names across.
    declare permissions {
        project:browse      "View the project and its issues"
        issue:create        "Create issues in the project"
        issue:edit          "Edit issue fields"
        issue:assign        "Assign issues to members"
        issue:transition    "Move issues through the workflow"
        issue:delete        "Delete issues"
        comment:write       "Comment on issues, and edit or delete your own"
        sprint:manage       "Create and manage sprints"
        project:administer  "Administer the project — settings, membership, roles, overrides"

        # ⚠️ The tenth, and the only one this move adds. A ROLE IS INSTALLATION-WIDE:
        # editing what Developer carries changes it in every project at once, which
        # is not a power `project:administer` can honestly contain — a person
        # administering one project would be editing everybody else's. It also gates
        # reading who holds what across the installation, which is a disclosure
        # surface rather than a convenience.
        access:administer   "Edit the roles every project shares, and see who holds what"

        # ⚠️ The eleventh, and installation-wide for the same reason the tenth is.
        # THE CONFIGURATION IS SHARED BY DESIGN — a workflow, a status and a scheme
        # belong to every project that uses them — so adding a transition changes
        # what everybody may do. `project:administer` cannot honestly contain that:
        # somebody administering one project would be editing everybody else's.
        #
        # It gates WRITES ONLY. `GET /api/configuration*` stays open to any
        # signed-in caller, because every picker in the product is built from it.
        configuration:administer "Edit the catalogs every project runs on — statuses, workflows, issue types, schemes"
    }

    # ── The three project roles ───────────────────────────────────────────────
    #
    # Each is the previous one plus something, and the growth is deliberate: a
    # person moved up a step should never lose anything, or "promote" becomes a
    # change nobody can predict.
    #
    # ⚠️ `assignable @PROJECT` is what stops any of them being handed out
    # installation-wide. It comes from the grammar and from nowhere else — deriving
    # it from the widest scope in the bundle would be right by accident for these
    # three and undefined for a role that bundles nothing.

    # Read-only, and may comment. Everything a person needs to follow work they are
    # not doing.
    declare role PROJECT_VIEWER assignable @PROJECT {
        @PROJECT  project:browse
        @PROJECT  comment:write
    }

    # Works on issues. Everything except deleting one and administering the project
    # — the two acts that are hard to undo and the two that are about the container
    # rather than about the work.
    declare role PROJECT_DEVELOPER assignable @PROJECT {
        @PROJECT  project:browse
        @PROJECT  comment:write
        @PROJECT  issue:create
        @PROJECT  issue:edit
        @PROJECT  issue:assign
        @PROJECT  issue:transition
        @PROJECT  sprint:manage
    }

    # Full control of the project, its settings and its membership.
    declare role PROJECT_ADMINISTRATOR assignable @PROJECT {
        @PROJECT  project:browse
        @PROJECT  comment:write
        @PROJECT  issue:create
        @PROJECT  issue:edit
        @PROJECT  issue:assign
        @PROJECT  issue:transition
        @PROJECT  issue:delete
        @PROJECT  sprint:manage
        @PROJECT  project:administer
    }

    # ── The way back in ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    #
    # An authorization system has a chicken-and-egg at its root: somebody needs
    # rights before anybody can be granted rights.
    #
    # ⚠️ DELIBERATELY NOT A SUPERUSER. It opens no project, reads no issue and
    # transitions nothing. Everything the tracker actually *does* still comes from a
    # project role somebody can edit and take away.
    #
    # ⚠️ IT CARRIES `configuration:administer` TOO, and that is a widening worth
    # naming. It used to carry exactly one permission and its whole story was "enough
    # to hand the powers back out". Editing the shared catalogs is not that — it is a
    # second installation-wide job that happens to have the same holder, because the
    # only global assignment this product ever makes is `MemberProvisioner` handing
    # this role to the first member. A role of its own would be more honest and would
    # reach nobody: nothing assigns a global role from a screen, so a second one would
    # sit in the catalog held by no one, which is the exact failure this cluster's
    # spec warns about.
    #
    # It stays separable where it matters: the two permissions are two switches on the
    # access screen, so an installation that wants the configuration in different hands
    # takes this one off here and grants it personally.
    #
    # There is deliberately no `@GLOBAL *`. A wildcard is convenient exactly where
    # convenience is worst.
    declare role GLOBAL_ACCESS_ADMINISTRATOR assignable @GLOBAL {
        @GLOBAL  access:administer
        @GLOBAL  configuration:administer
    }

    # ⚠️ THE ROLE IS DECLARED HERE AND ASSIGNED IN CODE, unlike Innoventa's, and the
    # difference is about where accounts come from. Innoventa writes
    # `assign subject ${innoventa.bootstrap.owner}` because it seeds a superuser row
    # in a migration and therefore knows an identifier before anybody signs in.
    # Tessera's members are PROVISIONED ON FIRST SIGN-IN from Identity's token, so at
    # the moment this file is read there is no identifier to name — and a placeholder
    # naming nobody would be a grant that quietly confers nothing.
    #
    # So `MemberProvisioner` gives it to the FIRST member ever provisioned. That is a
    # row, in the one home, carrying who and when; it is auditable and — unlike a
    # grant a file keeps re-asserting — it can be handed on and taken away.

    # ── The floors ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    #
    # ⚠️ DECLARATION ORDER IS WIDTH ORDER. The rank of each floor in the covering
    # chain is its position in this block and there is deliberately no rank column
    # anywhere. `parameter=projectId` is what lets a route spell its place into the
    # URL and be bound without naming a resource — which is why almost nothing in
    # this product needs an `AccessTargetResolver`.
    #
    # Why there is no @BOARD and no @ORGANIZATION is written into `TesseraScope`'s
    # own javadoc, beside the enum this block has to agree with.
    declare scopes {
        @GLOBAL   everything
        @PROJECT  place  parameter=projectId
        @SELF     'own-rows'
    }

    # ── Who holds the way back in ─────────────────────────────────────────────
    #
    # The one assignment written down rather than made on a screen, because it is
    # the one that cannot be made on a screen: the screen is behind it.
    #
    # ⚠️ THE HANDLE IS RESOLVED, AND IT HAS TO BE. `tessera.bootstrap.owner` is
    # whatever a person can write down BEFORE anybody has signed in — a member id, an
    # identity-provider subject (`SU`), or an email. Written straight through it
    # produced a row with subject_id = 'SU', and every grant here is keyed on a
    # MEMBER ID — so it parsed, projected, appeared on the access screen, and granted
    # ABSOLUTELY NOTHING. `PolicySeedStep` resolves it now and withdraws that row.
    #
    # ⚠️ AND IT IS APPLIED TWICE, because there are two moments it can be true.
    # Members arrive from Identity on first sign-in and the seed runs at the first
    # start, so whichever comes first has to work: the seed grants it if the owner
    # already exists, and `MemberProvisioner` grants it the moment they sign in. One
    # rule — "the configured owner holds it" — with two trigger points.
    #
    # ⚠️ An owner who never signs in holds nothing, and the boot says so rather than
    # leaving a row that looks like a grant.
    assign subject ${tessera.bootstrap.owner} {
        grants GLOBAL_ACCESS_ADMINISTRATOR @GLOBAL
    }
}
